Q: HTTP server behind IIS: pass authentication headers A: <p>A reliable solution is to write a custom <code>IHttpModule</code> that hooks into the <code>AuthorizeRequest</code> event, which fires after authentication, and sets a custom header via <code>ServerVariables</code>. ARR will then forward this header to your backend.</p>
<p><strong>1. Create <code>WindowsAuthHeaderModule.cs</code></strong> in an <code>App_Code</code> folder under your site root (e.g. <code>C:\inetpub\wwwroot\App_Code</code>):</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>using System;
using System.Web;

public class WindowsAuthHeaderModule : IHttpModule
{
    public void Init(HttpApplication context)
    {
        context.AuthorizeRequest += OnAuthorizeRequest;
    }

    private void OnAuthorizeRequest(object sender, EventArgs e)
    {
        var app = (HttpApplication)sender;
        var user = app.Context.User;
        var identity = user != null ? user.Identity : null;

        if (identity != null &amp;&amp; identity.IsAuthenticated)
        {
            var username = identity.Name;
            // Strip DOMAIN\ prefix if present
            if (username.Contains(&quot;\\&quot;))
                username = username.Split('\\')[1];

            // Setting HTTP_X_REMOTE_USER makes ARR forward it
            // as the X-Remote-User header to the backend
            app.Context.Request.ServerVariables.Set(&quot;HTTP_X_REMOTE_USER&quot;, username);
        }
    }

    public void Dispose() { }
}
</code></pre>
<p><strong>2. Register the module</strong> in your <code>web.config</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;configuration&gt;
    &lt;system.webServer&gt;
        &lt;modules&gt;
            &lt;add name=&quot;WindowsAuthHeaderModule&quot; type=&quot;WindowsAuthHeaderModule&quot; /&gt;
        &lt;/modules&gt;
        &lt;rewrite&gt;
            &lt;rules&gt;
                &lt;rule name=&quot;Reverse Proxy&quot; stopProcessing=&quot;true&quot;&gt;
                    &lt;match url=&quot;(.*)&quot; /&gt;
                    &lt;action type=&quot;Rewrite&quot; url=&quot;http://your-backend-server/{R:1}&quot; /&gt;
                &lt;/rule&gt;
            &lt;/rules&gt;
        &lt;/rewrite&gt;
    &lt;/system.webServer&gt;
&lt;/configuration&gt;
</code></pre>
<p><strong>How it works:</strong> Because the module subscribes to <code>AuthorizeRequest</code> (which runs after the <code>AuthenticateRequest</code> stage), <code>User.Identity</code> is populated by then. Setting a server variable with the <code>HTTP_</code> prefix causes IIS/ARR to forward it as an HTTP header to the backend. Your Linux backend can then read the <code>X-Remote-User</code> header.</p>
<p><em>Full step-by-step documentation for this approach (in the context of Orbeon Forms) is available at <a href="https://doc.orbeon.com/installation/iis" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://doc.orbeon.com/installation/iis</a>.</em></p>
